Q: Enable the &quot;Undo Move&quot; context menu after File.Move A: <p>Based on the very helpful <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79884751/enable-the-undo-move-context-menu-after-file-move/79884828#79884828">Luuk's answer</a>, I implemented a more specific need that I have. My goal is to move multiple files at once to a different folder, and create just one &quot;Undo Move&quot; entry for the whole operation. I.e. pressing Ctrl+Z one time, should move all the files back to the original folder. Luuk's answer explains that the Win32 <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/shell/manage" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>SHFileOperation</code></a> API supports multiple null-separated strings in the <code>pFrom</code> field. So I wrote a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/classes-and-structs/extension-methods" rel="nofollow noreferrer">static extension method</a> <code>MoveMany</code> for the <code>File</code> class, that can be used like this:</p>
<pre><code>File.MoveMany([@&quot;C:\Jupiter.jpg&quot;, @&quot;C:\Saturn.jpg&quot;], @&quot;C:\Archive&quot;, allowUndo: true);
</code></pre>
<p>Static extension methods are supported from C# 14 (.NET 10) and later.</p>
<p>Here is the implementation:</p>
<pre><code>static class FileExtensions
{
    extension(File)
    {
        public static void MoveMany(string[] sourceFiles,
            string destinationDirectory, bool allowUndo = false)
        {
            ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(sourceFiles);
            ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(destinationDirectory);

            for (int i = 0; i &lt; sourceFiles.Length; i++)
            {
                string source = sourceFiles[i] = Path.GetFullPath(sourceFiles[i]);
                if (!File.Exists(source))
                    throw new FileNotFoundException(
                        &quot;Could not find source file.&quot;, source);
            }

            destinationDirectory = Path.GetFullPath(destinationDirectory);
            if (!Directory.Exists(destinationDirectory))
                throw new DirectoryNotFoundException(
                    $&quot;Could not find destination directory.&quot;);

            ushort fFlags = FOF_NOCONFIRMATION | FOF_NOERRORUI | FOF_SILENT;
            if (allowUndo) fFlags |= FOF_ALLOWUNDO;

            SHFILEOPSTRUCT sh = new()
            {
                hwnd = IntPtr.Zero,
                wFunc = FO_MOVE,
                // SHFileOperation expects double-null terminated strings.
                // It supports multiple null-separated items.
                pFrom = String.Join(&quot;\0&quot;, sourceFiles) + &quot;\0\0&quot;,
                pTo = destinationDirectory + &quot;\0\0&quot;,
                fFlags = fFlags,
                fAnyOperationsAborted = false,
                hNameMappings = IntPtr.Zero,
                lpszProgressTitle = null
            };

            int result = SHFileOperation(ref sh);
            if (result != 0)
                throw new Win32Exception(result);

            if (sh.fAnyOperationsAborted)
                throw new OperationCanceledException(
                    &quot;File operation aborted by the shell.&quot;);
        }
    }

    // P/Invoke declarations
    [DllImport(&quot;shell32.dll&quot;, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)]
    private static extern int SHFileOperation(ref SHFILEOPSTRUCT lpFileOp);

    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)]
    private struct SHFILEOPSTRUCT
    {
        public IntPtr hwnd;
        public uint wFunc;
        [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPWStr)]
        public string pFrom;
        [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPWStr)]
        public string pTo;
        public ushort fFlags;
        [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.Bool)]
        public bool fAnyOperationsAborted;
        public IntPtr hNameMappings;
        [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPWStr)]
        public string lpszProgressTitle;
    }

    private const uint FO_MOVE = 0x0001;
    private const ushort FOF_ALLOWUNDO = 0x0040;
    private const ushort FOF_NOCONFIRMATION = 0x0010;
    private const ushort FOF_SILENT = 0x0004;
    private const ushort FOF_NOERRORUI = 0x0400;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to load structs from a text file with fixed multi-line records in C A: <blockquote>
<p>How can I load records that span multiple lines like this?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First, regardless of details, I recommend a <code>ReadTask()</code> helper function to encapsulate this task of reading 1 <em>record</em>.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Avoid being too tight on reading</strong></p>
<p>At <code>fgets()</code> time, avoid stingy read buffers.  I usually recommend a 2x expected size on read lines, when performance is not that great an issue.</p>
<p><code>fgets()</code> typically needs a buffer at least 1 longer that the maximum allowed to make it easy to determine if input is too long.</p>
<p>A tighter alternative is to read using the maximum size and then if the length of the received string fills the buffer and does not end with a <code>'\n'</code> , the next <code>fgetc()</code> best be a <code>'\n'</code> or <code>EOF</code>.  (Not shown below, yet a good improvement.)</p>
<p>Minor bug: <strong>off by 1</strong></p>
<p>OP currently has an  off by 1 issue.</p>
<p>Consider the below can write a <em>string</em> up to 499 in length and then a <code>'\n'</code>.  That is 500 characters.</p>
<pre><code>#define MAX_DESCRIPTION_SIZE 500
char description[MAX_DESCRIPTION_SIZE];
fprintf(data_file, &quot;%s\n&quot;, todo_list[i].description);
</code></pre>
<p>To read all that with 1 <code>fgets()</code> takes a buffer of 501.  As is, OP's code does not allow for proper reading of a maximal <em>lie</em> for <code>.description</code> as its max buffer size is 500 - one short.</p>
<p><code>line[strcspn(line, &quot;\n&quot;)] = '\0';</code> further complicates things if the last line of input is maximal and without a <code>'\n'</code> before the end-of-file.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong></p>
<p>Saving data in 1 <em>line</em> per record is better, yet if still determined to use three consecutive lines per records then always read three lines.</p>
<p>Look for long lines, end-of-file and only succeeded if all 3 read.</p>
<p>Later versions of this function might look for ASCII, <em>null characters</em>, variant line endings, other handling of long lines, versioning (old data format on one line, new data on 3 lines, ...</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// Return 1: Success,
// EOF: end-of-file/input error, 
// 0: Some failure: e.g. record too big.
int ReadTask(Task *t, FILE *inf) {
  static const size_t size[3] = { //
      sizeof t-&gt;title, sizeof t-&gt;description, sizeof t-&gt;date };
  static const size_t off[3] = { //
      offsetof(Task, title), offsetof(Task, description), offsetof(Task, date) };

  char buf[sizeof *t];  // Something bigger than one member.
  int retval = 1;

  for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    if (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, inf) == NULL) {
      return EOF;
    }

    // Lop off optional '\n'
    size_t len = strlen(buf);
    if (len &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; buf[len-1] == '\n') {
      buf[--len] = '\0';
    }

    char *member = (char*)t + off[i];
    if (len &gt;= size[i]) {
      *member = '\0';
      retval = 0;
    } else {
      strcpy(member, buf);
    }
  }

  return retval;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Direct Upload to S3 from Angular failing with 400 response A: <p>You are signing the URL with content_type:</p>
<pre><code>presigned_url = self.api_utility.presign_from(
    s3_bucket_name,
    object_key,
    content_type=content_type,
    object_method=&quot;put_object&quot;
)
</code></pre>
<p>So S3 expects the PUT request from angular  to include this exact header:
But Your Angular upload code is :</p>
<pre><code>uploadFileToS3(url: string, file: File) {
  return this.http.put(url, file, {
    observe: 'response',
    responseType: 'text' as 'json',
    withCredentials: false
  });
}

</code></pre>
<p>**You do not set Content-Type here
**</p>
<p>Correct fix (frontend + backend aligned)
When requesting the presigned URL, send content-type from frontend to backend.
Use the same Content-Type during PUT request to S3.</p>
